@chapter 18 | Fuels

@intro
Fuel questions ask which fuels are **natural** or **fossil**, which is **cleanest**, what **octane** and **cetane** numbers measure, how petrol and diesel engines differ, and what **biogas, LPG, CNG** and **gasohol** are made of. A few cover anti-knock agents, refinery products, hydrogen storage and India's oil policy. It contains **{pyqs} PYQs and {ones} one-liners**.
@end

@section 18.1 | Natural and Fossil Fuels

@q 29:1-3

@table
Fuel | Type | Main component
**Petroleum** (crude oil) | **Natural**, fossil | Mixture of hydrocarbons
Coal | Natural, fossil | Carbon
**Natural gas** | Natural, fossil; **cleanest fossil fuel** | **Methane** (80-90%)
Coke, coal gas, tar | **Derived** (made from coal) | -
Water gas | Derived | CO + H_{2}
Producer gas | Derived | CO + N_{2}
@end

**Fossil fuels** formed over millions of years from buried plants and animals: coal, petroleum and natural gas. Natural gas burns most completely and gives the least CO_{2}, soot and SO_{2} per unit of energy.

@section 18.2 | Engines, Octane and Cetane

@q 29:4-7

@table
Feature | Petrol engine | Diesel engine
Ignition | **Spark plug** | **Compression** (hot compressed air ignites the fuel)
Fuel-air mixing | **Carburettor** (now fuel injection) | **Fuel injector** sprays diesel into hot air
Quality rating | **Octane number** (anti-knock) | **Cetane number** (ease of ignition)
Efficiency | Lower | Higher, more pulling power: trucks, buses, tractors, ships
@end

**Knocking** is the early, uneven burning of fuel in an engine. **Octane number** measures petrol's resistance to knocking (iso-octane = 100, n-heptane = 0). **Tetraethyl lead** was once added to raise octane, but lead is toxic; India sells only **unleaded** petrol since 2000.

@q 29:20-21

@section 18.3 | Biogas, LPG and CNG

@q 29:8-11

**Biogas** (gobar gas) is made by **anaerobic fermentation** of cattle dung and organic waste by bacteria. It is about **55-70% methane** and **30-40% carbon dioxide**, with traces of hydrogen sulphide. The leftover slurry is good manure. The **GOBARdhan** scheme (2018) and **SATAT** (compressed biogas, 2018) promote it.

@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Pre) 2015
Who among the following invented 'Gobar Gas' system?
(a) C.V. Raman
(b) J.C. Bose
(c) C.B. Desai
(d) H. Khorana
@ans (*)
@note The gobar gas plant was developed by **S.V. Desai** of IARI (and popularised by KVIC in the 1960s); the option printed "C.B. Desai" does not match exactly, so the source marks the question (*). (c) is the intended answer.
@end
@src 29:12

@q 29:13-15

@table
Gas | Composition | Note
**LPG** (liquefied petroleum gas, "Indane") | **Butane + propane** (some ethane), with **ethyl mercaptan** added for smell | Cooking; heavier than air, so leaks collect near the floor; **Ujjwala** scheme (2016)
**CNG** (compressed natural gas) | **Methane** (80-90%) at 200-250 bar | Vehicles; lighter than air, cleaner than petrol and diesel
**PNG** (piped natural gas) | Methane | City gas for kitchens
LNG | Natural gas cooled to -162°C | Imported by ship
Biogas | Methane + CO_{2} | Rural fuel
@end

@section 18.4 | Alcohol Fuels and Hydrogen

@q 29:16-18

**Gasohol** is petrol (gasoline) blended with **ethanol**. **Brazil** made blending compulsory from 1976 and uses sugarcane ethanol widely. **India** reached **20% ethanol blending (E20)** in 2025, five years ahead of the original 2030 target, using molasses, sugarcane juice, maize and damaged grain.

@q 29:22

**Hydrogen** can be stored safely in solid **metal hydrides** (palladium, magnesium, alloys), which soak it up at low temperature and release it on warming. India's **National Green Hydrogen Mission** (2023) targets 5 million tonnes a year by 2030.

@section 18.5 | Petroleum Refining

@q 29:19

@table
Fraction (fractional distillation, low to high boiling point) | Use
Petroleum gas (LPG) | Cooking fuel
Petrol (gasoline) | Cars
Naphtha | Petrochemicals, fertiliser
Kerosene | Jet fuel (ATF), lamps
Diesel | Trucks, generators
Lubricating oil, paraffin wax | Machines, candles
**Bitumen / asphalt** (residue) | **Road surfacing**, waterproofing
@end

@q 29:23

**Hydrocarbon Vision 2025** (1999-2000) was India's 25-year plan for the oil and gas sector: energy security, more domestic exploration and refining, and **strategic storage** of petroleum. India now keeps **strategic petroleum reserves** in rock caverns at Visakhapatnam, Mangaluru and Padur.

@trap
**Petroleum is a natural fuel**; coke, coal gas and tar are made from coal. **Natural gas is the cleanest fossil fuel.** **Octane → petrol, cetane → diesel.** A **carburettor** belongs to a **petrol** engine. **Biogas = methane + CO_{2}** by fermentation. **LPG = butane + propane**; **CNG = methane**. **Gasohol = petrol + ethanol.** **Asphalt** is the refinery residue.
@end

